window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["25"]={"https://theconversation.com/budget-2026-gives-households-a-boost-but-businesses-get-the-biggest-break-295949":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"c84218104bc534079d44a00db66a484e0ba08027ab9db168a211ee729029734a","read":"https://theconversation.com/budget-2026-gives-households-a-boost-but-businesses-get-the-biggest-break-295949","bytes":183286,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Budget 2026 gives households a boost, but businesses get the biggest break","by":[{"n":"Preetika Joshi","r":"Assistant Professor of Accounting, McGill University"}],"pub":"2026-11-10T16:10:13Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["In its ",["November budget","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/pdf/budget-2026-in-brief.pdf"],", the federal government promises households “a boost today and a bridge to tomorrow.” But above all, the budget is a bet on business investment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Its centrepiece is the ",["Productivity Mega Deduction","https://www.canada.ca/en/department-finance/news/2026/09/government-of-canada-introduces-new-productivity-mega-deduction-to-boost-canadas-advantage-as-the-most-competitive-g7-country-for-new-business-inve.html"],", a tax incentive that allows businesses to write off the full cost of eligible capital investments. It’s expected to cost ",["$36 billion over five years","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/pdf/budget-2026.pdf#page=121"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["By my calculations, only about 22 cents of every new dollar goes to affordability measures. These include not only direct household assistance but also housing, skills and immigration."]},{"k":"p","s":["As a tax policy researcher at McGill University, I read budgets for how policy choices show up in everyday economic life. Looking beyond the headline announcements reveals who benefits, how much they receive and when that assistance arrives."]},{"k":"h","s":["The highlights for households"]},{"k":"p","s":["For many households, the biggest immediate effect is the ",["continuation of existing support","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/chap3-en.html"],", including rent subsidies for low-income tenants, provincial funding for home care and mental health and food bank support."]},{"k":"p","s":["Four changes stand out. The ",["Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit","https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/child-family-benefits/canada-groceries-essentials-benefit.html"]," — formerly the GST credit — will provide another one-time top-up in early 2027, worth up to $339 for individuals and $679 for couples with two children, paid in full to most households earning under about $46,000."]},{"k":"p","s":["The new ",["Build Canada Workers Benefit","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/pdf/budget-2026-annex-4.pdf#page=36"]," pays the lowest earners up to $675 more and reaches workers earning under $40,000 (about $52,000 for families), but from 2028 they will receive it only after filing their tax return."]},{"k":"p","s":["The federal caregiver credit becomes refundable from 2027, meaning it’s paid even to people who owe no tax. About 180,000 low-income caregivers who currently receive no benefit will be able to claim up to $1,260 annually."]},{"k":"p","s":["The most consequential change may not touch wallets at all. From the 2027 tax year, everyone will have a May 15 deadline to file and pay their taxes. This gives most taxpayers two additional weeks to pay, but self-employed people will have a month less to file."]},{"k":"p","s":["To illustrate how the changes could affect people in different circumstances, I have constructed five fictional household scenarios, based on the budget’s eligibility rules and benefit calculations."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scenario 1: A university student in Toronto"]},{"k":"p","s":["Arjun, 20, rents in Toronto and earns $25,000 from part-time and summer jobs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Arjun will get the full grocery top-up of $339. Although he earns a low wage, ",["he will not benefit from the higher workers benefit","https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/topics/about-your-tax-return/tax-return/completing-a-tax-return/deductions-credits-expenses/line-45300-canada-workers-benefit-cwb/who-is-eligible.html"]," because full-time students are excluded."]},{"k":"p","s":["He could benefit from renewed rent subsidies only if he already receives them. He will also be able to visit national museums for free, as admission becomes permanently free for people under 25."]},{"k":"p","s":["What’s in it for him: a single payment of $339 and free museum admission."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scenario 2: A low-income family in Winnipeg"]},{"k":"p","s":["Kwame and Sophie rent a house in Winnipeg and have two young children. Sophie earns $18,000 annually, while Kwame earns $2,000 from part-time work alongside caring for his mother, who has dementia."]},{"k":"p","s":["The family will receive the full $679 grocery top-up, and, by my calculations, $200 more from the new workers benefit, since Kwame’s $2,000 in earnings do not reduce the benefit. But from 2028, that benefit will come only after they file their tax return, not in payments during the year."]},{"k":"p","s":["The biggest gain is for Kwame. He has long been eligible for the caregiver credit but with too little income, he could never use it. Now that it is refundable, he receives the full $1,260 a year."]},{"k":"p","s":["Their children will also be able to visit the Canadian Museum for Human Rights for free."]},{"k":"p","s":["What’s in it for them: about $2,100 more in support the first year (with some of it paid later) and free museum admission for their children."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scenario 3: A business-owning household in Mississauga"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Chows are homeowners in Mississauga, Ont., with a combined annual income of $120,000. The father, Tony, runs a catering business as a sole proprietor."]},{"k":"p","s":["They earn too much to qualify for the grocery top-up or the workers benefit. But Tony is the kind of business owner the budget’s centrepiece is built for."]},{"k":"p","s":["If he buys $40,000 of new kitchen equipment, the Productivity Mega Deduction lets him deduct the full cost in the year he buys it, rather than over several years. At a 30 per cent tax rate, that cuts his tax bill that year by about $12,000."]},{"k":"p","s":["The new May 15 deadline gives him two more weeks to pay, but a month less to file."]},{"k":"p","s":["What’s in it for them: about $12,000 less tax in the year Tony invests, more time to pay but less time to file."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scenario 4: A retired pensioner in Sudbury"]},{"k":"p","s":["Lucien, 73, lives in Sudbury, Ont., and receives $45,000 annually in pension income."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lucien will receive the full $339 grocery supplement, but no new support through Old Age Security or the Guaranteed Income Supplement."]},{"k":"p","s":["Because little tax is withheld from his pension income, he owes approximately $4,000 annually. He must pay this in four quarterly instalments of about $1,000. Starting in 2028, the threshold for mandatory instalments rises to $7,500, allowing him to pay his tax bill in full when he files in May."]},{"k":"p","s":["What’s in it for him: a single payment of $339 and less paperwork."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scenario 5: A self-employed artist in Halifax"]},{"k":"p","s":["Daniel, 34, is a self-employed illustrator in Halifax, earning $62,000 annually."]},{"k":"p","s":["The budget’s opening pages promise to make up to $45,000 of artistic earnings tax free. However, the measure is only a proposal to consult on a ",["non-refundable Canada Creative Credit","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/chap4-en.html"]," that, combined with the basic personal amount that everyone already gets, would cover up to $45,000 of artistic business income."]},{"k":"p","s":["If implemented, it could save Daniel up to approximately $4,000 annually in federal tax, based on my calculations. But details are not expected until spring 2027, and whether an illustrator’s work will qualify as “original and creative artistic works” is still to be decided."]},{"k":"p","s":["What’s in it for him: a potential tax benefit, but for now, a promise rather than a payment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Who benefits most?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The budget’s assistance is primarily aimed at the lower-income households, particularly workers and caregivers. Much of it, however, is either a one-time payment or will not arrive until 2027 or later."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seniors, students and salaried middle-income families get little new support. Meanwhile, the biggest tax break goes to businesses that invest, and it pays off only for those that are profitable."]},{"k":"p","s":["The government is betting that greater business investment will raise incomes over time, and eventually benefit Canadians more broadly. But those gains are not guaranteed, and households facing high living costs need relief now."]},{"k":"p","s":["For now, they get a boost. The bridge to tomorrow is still under construction."]}],"chars":6662},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"c5c798da14319bf489c8cfc1a07955bc89b8f86c8919819952b8523bf04d32e0","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea","bytes":65611,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Papua New Guinea travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-06T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-17T15:35:08+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all but essential travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Hela and Southern Highlands Provinces due to the high risk of tribal fighting"]},{"k":"li","s":["Enga Province in the Highlands, except Wabag District"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Interruptions to water and electricity supply"]},{"k":"p","s":["Papua New Guinea can experience interruptions in the supply of water and electricity to urban centres. The water supplier to Port Moresby, Water PNG, completed some planned maintenance works in mid-August."]},{"k":"p","s":["Consider personal preparedness and regularly check media updates from ",["Water PNG","https://www.facebook.com/p/Water-PNG-100069303425822/"]," for the latest information."]},{"k":"h","s":["Global travel impacts due to escalation in the Middle East"]},{"k":"p","s":["Escalation in the Middle East has caused widespread travel disruption, including airspace closures, delayed and cancelled flights."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel plans may be affected, even if your destination is not in the Middle East. Before you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check travel advice for any countries or territories you are transiting through"]},{"k":"li","s":["check for the latest updates from your airline or tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["review your travel insurance policy for coverage"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local and international media for the latest information and sign up for travel advice email alerts"]},{"k":"h","s":["Enga Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["Following wide-spread intercommunal violence in Wapenamanda area of Enga Province, the provincial government has declared all districts except Wabag District, which remains relatively safe, as ‘fighting zones’. The authorities report deaths, destruction of property, and the closure of public services. Checkpoints, random searches, and curfews have been introduced."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Papua New Guinea’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Papua New Guinea set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Papua New Guinean High Commission in the UK","https://www.pnghighcomm.org.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Papua New Guinea, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid British passport or ",["proof of right of abode","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit Papua New Guinea. Visas can be obtained in advance online or at a Papua New Guinea (PNG) Embassy or High Commission. Tourist/visitor visas can also be obtained on arrival at Jacksons International Airport in Port Moresby."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Apply for a Papua New Guinea visa online","https://ica.gov.pg/"]," before you travel using the ‘Online Services’ tab on the Immigration & Citizenship Services Authority (ICSA) website."]},{"k":"p","s":["PNG has recently reintroduced visa on arrival for certain categories of traveller. Details are available on the ",["ICSA website","https://ica.gov.pg/visa-information/visa-on-arrival"],". You may need to show evidence of an onward flight ticket."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Papua New Guinea guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/175/papua-new-guinea#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Digital Arrival Card"]},{"k":"p","s":["From 1 October 2025 all travellers to Papua New Guinea must complete and submit online a ",["Digital Arrival Card","https://pngdac.ica.gov.pg/"],". This is free of charge and should be submitted within 72 hours of arrival."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Papua New Guinea, see:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["prohibited goods","https://customs.gov.pg/trade.php?q=prohibitions"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["duty-free allowances for travellers","https://customs.gov.pg/border.php?q=travellers-concessions"]]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to enter Papua New Guinea with many fruits, vegetables and animal products due to local quarantine controls."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Papua New Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Papua New Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Papua New Guinea, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Tribal fighting"]},{"k":"p","s":["Outbreaks of tribal fighting are common across Papua New Guinea, including tourist areas. Ethnic disputes can quickly escalate and lead to widespread destruction of property, disruption of normal services and serious injury. Tribal fighters may be armed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Although tribal fighters do not normally attack foreign nationals, you should stay away from areas where there is fighting."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay alert, monitor local media and consult local contacts such as your hotel before you travel to a new area. Be particularly cautious in the suburbs of Port Moresby and the Highlands Region. See ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk of serious crime in the capital, Port Moresby, and in the cities of Lae and Mount Hagen. Suburbs of towns and cities can be particularly dangerous."]},{"k":"p","s":["High-risk crime areas include the:"]},{"k":"li","s":["area around Parliament House in the Port Moresby suburb of Waigani, particularly outside of working hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["highway between Lae and Nadzab Airport"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a serious risk of bag-snatching and robbery. Criminals often use machetes (‘bush knives’) and firearms in assaults and thefts. They target people who are using banks and ATMs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Walking after dark is particularly dangerous in Port Moresby and other urban centres."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take precautions such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["not displaying valuables like laptops, cameras and mobile phones"]},{"k":"li","s":["not wearing expensive-looking jewellery"]},{"k":"li","s":["carrying only small amounts of cash – avoid withdrawing a lot of money, particularly at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping valuables secure (for example, in a hotel safe)"]},{"k":"li","s":["not travelling alone or at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitoring the media for possible new security risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Carjacking and other types of vehicle crime are common. Criminals use roadblocks outside towns to stop and loot vehicles and then attack the occupants."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan travel in these areas, particularly after dark, take great care and consider travelling in convoy or using a security escort. Always lock car doors and keep windows up."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public buses and taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Armed criminals have attacked and robbed passengers on public buses (known as ‘PMVs’) and in taxis. There have also been incidents of rape on Port Moresby PMVs. Many PMVs and taxis are not roadworthy."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rape and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Rape and sexual assault are common in Papua New Guinea. Stay alert and leave travel plans with friends, relatives or reliable local contacts. See ",["advice for women travelling abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Kokoda Track"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been serious attacks and robberies along the Kokoda Track. Although community leaders have assured tourists of their safety and wellbeing while walking the Kokoda Track, you should take care. Avoid walking independently and travel with guides from trusted travel companies. You can get details from the ",["Papua New Guinea Tourism Authority","https://www.papuanewguinea.travel/"]," or the Kokoda Track Authority."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnapping"]},{"k":"p","s":["Organised gangs have abducted people and forced them to open office safes while others are held captive until a ransom has been paid. A foreign national was kidnapped in a remote part of Southern Highlands Province in 2023. Be vigilant and leave travel plans with friends, relatives or reliable local contacts."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Methanol poisoning"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been deaths and cases of serious illness caused by alcoholic drinks containing methanol in Papua New Guinea."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even small amounts of methanol can kill. It is not possible to identify methanol in alcoholic drinks by taste or smell. See ",["Travel Aware Drink Spiking and Methanol Poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," for information about how to reduce the risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seek urgent medical attention if you or someone you are travelling with show the ",["signs of methanol poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," after drinking."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Unexploded weapons"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are unexploded World War 2 weapons in Papua New Guinea, particularly along the Kokoda Track, at Milne Bay and Rabaul. If you are concerned, get advice from a trusted travel company for the areas you’re visiting."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is a serious offence to use, possess or traffic illegal drugs, including marijuana. If convicted, you can get a long prison sentence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity between men is illegal. People who are convicted face up to 14 years imprisonment. Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to participate in outdoor or adventure activities, consider getting advice on safety from a trusted travel company. You can get information from the ",["Papua New Guinea Tourism Authority","https://www.papuanewguinea.travel/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Papua New Guinea, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Papua New Guinea for the first 6 months after you arrive. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["rules for using a foreign driving licence in Papua New Guinea","https://www.rta.gov.pg/licences-approvals/driver-licensing/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Roads are in a poor state of repair, especially in rural areas, and driving is often erratic."]},{"k":"p","s":["Following a road traffic accident, crowds can form quickly and become violent towards those they consider responsible. If you are involved in an accident, do not remain at the scene. Go to the nearest safe place before reporting the incident to the police."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Delays and cancellations of international and domestic flights often happen. Check with your airline before travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Papua New Guinea is in an active seismic region known as the ‘Ring of Fire’. Volcanic eruptions, earthquakes and tsunamis are possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes are a serious risk in Papua New Guinea. Seismic and volcanic activity is more likely near Rabaul in East New Britain Province, Kimbe in West New Britain Province and on Manam Island in Madang Province."]},{"k":"p","s":["There was a 6.9 magnitude earthquake in the New Britain Region, Papua New Guinea at 06:04:39 local time, 5 Apr 2025. The advice is to follow emergency instructions from local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tsunamis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes can be followed by tsunamis. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after a tsunami","https://www.ready.gov/tsunamis"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Papua New Guinea has many active and dormant volcanoes. You should check with your travel provider or airline and seek advice from local authorities before travelling to affected areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["In November 2023, Mount Ulawun volcano erupted on the island of New Britain and caused flight cancellations and the evacuation of over 5,000 people. It has shown further low-level activity since then, most recently in March 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["Increased volcanic activity at Mount Bagana in Bougainville in 2023 resulted in widespread displacement of local residents. Some low-level activity continues."]},{"k":"p","s":["Manam Island volcano erupted in 2018, forcing thousands to flee to the mainland. It has shown further low-level activity since then, most recently in 2024."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tropical cyclones"]},{"k":"p","s":["The tropical cyclone season normally runs from November to May. Monitor local and international weather updates from the ",["World Meteorological Organization","https://severeweather.wmo.int/tc/au/index.html"]," and the Australian Bureau of Meteorology."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of flooding and landslides, especially in rural areas and during monsoon season from November to May. Exceptionally high tides (‘king tides’) can cause flooding in coastal areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Damage caused by heavy rain can make travel difficult."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Papua New Guinea. It only covers regions where the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Enga, Hela and Southern Highlands Provinces"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to Hela, Southern Highlands and Enga Provinces (except Wabag District) due to the high risk of tribal fighting."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to travel there, be cautious, stay away from large crowds and gatherings and consider getting private security advice. Pay attention to local advice on curfews and other restrictions which may be imposed at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be particularly cautious and stay away from, the area around the Porgera gold mine, which has been subject to violent landowner disputes."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["Tribal fighting","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Papua New Guinea-Indonesia border"]},{"k":"p","s":["The land border between Papua New Guinea and Indonesia stretches for around 760km and is poorly defined. There are sometimes armed clashes along the border between the Indonesian military and the Organisasi Papua Merdeka (OPM, Free Papua Movement)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Papua New Guinea government has defence units at its Wutung border post in Sandaun Province. Be extremely cautious and be prepared for possible sudden closure of the border crossing."]},{"k":"h","s":["Bougainville Island"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bougainville Island has been calm since a 2001 peace agreement ended separatist fighting. You should still be cautious, particularly in central and southern Bougainville. There is a risk of lawlessness and violence. The autonomous Bougainville government has designated the mountainous area in central Bougainville around the old Panguna mine a ‘no go zone’. Do not travel to it without prior authorisation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 111 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Papua New Guinea","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/175/papua-new-guinea#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Go to TravelHealthPro to see ",["what health risks you’ll face in Papua New Guinea","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/175/papua-new-guinea#General_Information"]," including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zika virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["tuberculosis"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria and dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["chikungunya"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Papua New Guinea. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare facilities in Papua New Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities in Papua New Guinea are very basic. Hospitals often run out of basic drugs and supplies and suffer from power shortages. Evacuation by air ambulance to Australia is available in more serious cases. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Papua New Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/papua-new-guinea-doctors"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Papua New Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 111"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 110"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 112"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Papua%20New%20Guinea"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Papua%20New%20Guinea"]," in Papua New Guinea"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Papua New Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/breavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Papua New Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/papua-new-guinea-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/papua-new-guinea/british-high-commission-port-moresby"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/papua-new-guinea/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Papua New Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-papua-new-guinea"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":18805,"change":"Updated information about interruptions to the supply of water and electricity (‘Warnings and insurance’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sierra-leone":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"2b274ea24751d94846e59dec1070e1c372977b210113933fc3a04d3acde28222","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/sierra-leone","bytes":59936,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Sierra Leone travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-19T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-06-19T15:34:37+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sierra-leone/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Sierra Leone’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Sierra Leone set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Sierra Leone High Commission in the UK","https://www.slhc-uk.org/?page_id=60"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Sierra Leone, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date of your visa application."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to enter Sierra Leone."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get a visa to visit or for business travel either:"]},{"k":"li","s":["when you arrive in Sierra Leone for 80 US dollars, paid in cash in US dollars"]},{"k":"li","s":["before you travel – apply online for an e-visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Single entry visas are valid for 90 days from date of issuing for visits up to a maximum of 30 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["Multiple entry visas are valid for 365 days from date of issuing and are valid for multiple separate trips for up to a maximum of 30 days for each visit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling for a purpose other than a visit or business, you must get a visa before you travel. Contact the ",["Sierra Leone High Commission in the UK","https://www.slhc-uk.org/?page_id=60"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Sierra Leone, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Sierra Leone guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/195/sierra-leone#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health travel declaration"]},{"k":"p","s":["All travellers entering or leaving Sierra Leone must complete a health declaration before travel using the ",["Sierra Leone Health Travel Portal.","https://poep.npha.gov.sl/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["You must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["complete the form online within 72 hours before travel"]},{"k":"li","s":["ensure your travel details are accurate, including your seat number where possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep your confirmation QR code or reference number and present it if requested"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be required to undergo health screening on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you do not comply, you may face delays or be refused entry or departure."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola Screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a public health measure, Sierra Leone has introduced screening for arriving travellers. You should expect enhanced health screening measures on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["The World Health Organisation (WHO) has ",["declared this a Public Health Emergency of International Concern","https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Sierra Leone","https://www.nra.gov.sl/import-and-export/customs-duty"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Export of precious stones"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have an export licence to leave Sierra Leone with precious stones. Check the quality of any gems or minerals you buy. Any deals that appear too good to be true probably are."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Sierra Leone"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Sierra Leone, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Following the end of the civil war in 2002, the political situation in Sierra Leone has improved. However, the political situation can change and protests can turn violent. Avoid any political gatherings or demonstrations and follow any curfews or restrictions. Remain vigilant as the security situation could change quickly."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["In a small number of incidents, British nationals have been robbed, sometimes at knifepoint. Robberies have taken place in Congo Cross, Wilkinson Road, Lumley Beach, Aberdeen and at beach resorts along the Freetown peninsula and surrounding islands. Crime rates in Sierra Leone are comparatively low, however incidents of violent and organised crime are rising. Exercise vigilance and avoid walking alone at night."]},{"k":"p","s":["The greatest risk to short-stay visitors is from pickpocketing and mugging in Freetown. If you’re staying for a longer period, make sure you secure your property and employ guards."]},{"k":"p","s":["To reduce your personal risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid crowds and political demonstrations"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not display or carry expensive items or large sums of money"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking alone after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["familiarise yourself with your surroundings and stay alert"]},{"k":"li","s":["arrange your transport in advance"]},{"k":"li","s":["check your hotel safe is secure"]},{"k":"p","s":["Public transport drivers and passengers have robbed foreign visitors. Avoid travelling in taxis, minibuses (‘poda-podas’), motorised rickshaws (‘kekes’) or motorbikes (‘ocadas’)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Reporting crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Sierra Leone Police charge foreign nationals a fee for police reports. They will not issue a crime reference number, which is needed for most insurance claims, without a police report. Do not pay the police directly. Make your payment to the Sierra Leone Police Revenue Generation Fund account at the Bank of Sierra Leone and get a receipt."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no charge if you want to report a crime and do not need a crime reference number."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sierra Leone has a tolerant, multi-religion culture. Always respect local traditions, customs, laws and religions and make sure your actions do not cause offence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry ID such as a passport or residence permit. The police will usually accept a copy of your passport’s photo page and visa stamp."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not become involved with drugs of any kind. There are heavy penalties for drug trafficking, including prison sentences. Prison conditions are harsh."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Diamond smuggling"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are heavy penalties for diamond smuggling, including prison sentences."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Land ownership disputes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Land registry records are sometimes disputed and can lead to land disputes, where more than one person claims rights to the same land. Disputes can lead to threats of violence. If you are concerned about land ownership, seek independent legal advice and contact the Anti-Land Grabbing Unit of the Sierra Leone Police. The British High Commission cannot intervene with disputes or investigate ownership claims."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking photographs and filmmaking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not take pictures of military installations or any other official government buildings, including prisons and courthouses. There have been instances where individuals have been arrested for doing so."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relations between men are illegal in Sierra Leone, even if they are consensual. Penalties can include imprisonment. Same-sex relations are not illegal between women. All travellers should exercise caution given the much more conservative culture than UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Adoption in Sierra Leone"]},{"k":"p","s":["The UK does not recognise adoption orders from Sierra Leone. If you are planning to adopt a child in Sierra Leone, make sure you comply with Sierra Leone’s adoption laws. The ",["Ministry of Social Welfare, Gender and Children’s Affairs","https://mosw.gov.sl/"]," is responsible for adoptions in Sierra Leone."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Water sports and swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strong ocean currents along the coast in Sierra Leone, and no lifeguards on any beaches. You go into the sea at your own risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get local advice about sea conditions before you swim or do any water sports. Health and safety standards for water sports operators are usually lower than the UK. Check safety credentials in advance and always ask for a life jacket."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beware of hospital waste, including needles, on Lumley Beach."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Sierra Leone, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Sierra Leone for up to one month after you arrive. If you also get the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/get-an-idp"],", you can drive for the length of the IDP’s validity. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re staying longer or living in Sierra Leone, you must get a Sierra Leone driving licence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions and safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Serious traffic accidents are common and road travel across the country comes with risks, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["poor standards of vehicle maintenance"]},{"k":"li","s":["people driving erratically, particularly motorbike riders, and ignoring road regulations"]},{"k":"li","s":["roads with little or no lighting"]},{"k":"li","s":["vehicles with no lights, and pedestrians and animals on the road"]},{"k":"li","s":["damage to roads during the rainy season"]},{"k":"li","s":["high traffic levels, increasing the risk of collisions"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are travelling by road, particularly outside the Freetown peninsula, you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["plan your journey"]},{"k":"li","s":["try to complete your travel during daylight hours as risks are higher at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel in convoy if possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep your employer, friends and family updated on your progress"]},{"k":"li","s":["use well-maintained vehicles"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel with your doors locked"]},{"k":"li","s":["be alert to groups stopping your car under false pretences"]},{"k":"p","s":["British High Commission staff avoid road travel outside the Freetown peninsula at night. They do not use local taxis, minibuses (‘poda-podas’), motorised riskshaw (‘kekes’) or motorbikes (‘ocadas’)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The mobile network is unreliable, particularly in remote areas. If you do manage to communicate, the emergency response is unreliable. If you have an accident, mobs of people can form around your car. You need a plan for what to do in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sierra Leone’s borders with Guinea and Liberia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sierra Leone is bordered to the north and east by Guinea, and to the south by Liberia. There will be security personnel at these borders. Make sure you have your paperwork ready if you are crossing by land. Stay calm and patient while you wait for your paperwork to be processed and be prepared for disruption and short-notice border closures."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Checkpoints and unofficial roadblocks"]},{"k":"p","s":["Officers in uniform control official checkpoints with marked barriers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Children sometimes put a rope across the road and ask for a small donation for mending the road. These unofficial roadblocks are most common at the weekend and on the roads to tourist beaches in the Western Area."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["UK Air Safety List (ASL)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list"]," lists all known airlines in Sierra Leone that do not meet international safety standards and are banned from operating commercial air services to, from, and within the UK. Check the UK Air Safety List when considering which airlines to fly with. The list is maintained by the Department for Transport, based on advice from the ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.caa.co.uk/Commercial-Industry/Airlines/Licensing/Requirements-and-guidance/Third-Country-Operator-Certificates"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel to and from Lungi airport"]},{"k":"p","s":["Lungi airport is on the far side of a wide estuary across from Freetown. The journey time from Freetown to the airport is around 3 hours by road. There are no car hire facilities at the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["British High Commission staff do not travel by road after dark and rarely take this route in the daytime due to the risk of accidents."]},{"k":"p","s":["Let someone know your expected arrival time and what action they should take if you do not arrive at that time."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airport hotels rooms are extremely limited and in high demand."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Airport water taxis and ferries"]},{"k":"p","s":["The 2 main water taxi operators, ",["Sierra Leone Sea Coach Express Africa","https://www.seacoachexpress.com/sierra-leone/"]," and ",["SeaBird Water Taxi","https://seabirdsl.com/"],", run a reliable service between Freetown and Lungi. It takes 30 to 45 minutes depending on the size of the boat."]},{"k":"p","s":["The one-hour ferry service between Lungi Tagrin and Kissy Town only runs when there is enough demand."]},{"k":"p","s":["Water taxis are scheduled to align with specific flight arrivals and departures, and also include a courtesy bus between Lungi airport and the beach departure or arrival point in Lungi."]},{"k":"p","s":["During the rainy season from May to October, rough sea conditions may cause delays or cancellations. Water taxis have navigational aids, night lights and life jackets for passengers. However they have limited rescue capability if you fall overboard."]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid using the unregulated or privately operated boats such as wooden pirogues and canoes, which do not have lights or safety equipment. British High Commission staff do not travel on small boats."]},{"k":"h","s":["Phone services and utilities"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no country-wide coverage from mobile network providers in Sierra Leone. Some areas have no mobile reception. There’s no public telephone system, and landline connections are mainly disused. Calls between Sierra Leone mobile phones and Sierra Leone landline phones are not possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mains water is limited. Mains power is unreliable and extended outages are common. Power surges, especially in the rainy season, are common – you may want to use power surge protectors. Hotels and rented villas have generators and private water supplies."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 117 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance services are unreliable in Freetown and unavailable throughout most of the country. Staff are not usually trained paramedics and often have little or no medical equipment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some private hospitals, particularly in Freetown, operate their own commercial ambulances. Injured or seriously ill travellers may need to take a taxi or private vehicle to the nearest major hospital rather than wait for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Sierra Leone","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/195/sierra-leone#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Sierra Leone","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/195/sierra-leone#General_Information"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["cholera"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["rabies"]},{"k":"p","s":["Water-borne diseases, including cholera, are endemic in Sierra Leone with outbreaks, particularly during the rainy season, in areas where there is poor sanitation. You should drink or use only boiled or bottled water and avoid ice in drinks."]},{"k":"p","s":["As of 2023, UNAIDS estimate that around 77,000 people in Sierra Leone were living with HIV. You should take normal precautions to avoid exposure to HIV/AIDS."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in Sierra Leone for business, check what medical support your employer can provide if you fall ill."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with the ",["Federal Office of Public Health","https://dhse.gov.sl/"]," that your medication is legal in Sierra Leone."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Sierra Leone"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Sierra Leone","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/sierra-leone-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hospitals, medical facilities and services in Sierra Leone are severely limited. The standard of care is much lower than in the UK, including basic services such as blood screening. Many facilities, particularly outside of Freetown, only accept cash payments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical supplies are unreliable and limited. Carry basic medical supplies and adequate supplies of prescription medication. Always carry your prescription medication in original packaging, along with your doctor’s prescription."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad, medical evacuation and repatriation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Sierra Leone"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 117 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Sierra%20Leone"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Sierra%20Leone"]," in Sierra Leone"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Sierra Leone","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/sierra-leone-bereavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Sierra Leone","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/sierra-leone-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/sierra-leone/british-high-commission-freetown"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sierra-leone/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Sierra Leone","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-sierra-leone"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":18565,"change":"New information about completing a health travel declaration (‘Entry Requirements’ page.)"},"https://theconversation.com/how-populist-parties-around-the-world-use-the-same-playbook-to-win-on-social-media-295561":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-18T01:12Z","sha":"a3d2ceb04c123e4968b9b73799030773ce70074f56b9640ee2c15a3fe6017349","read":"https://theconversation.com/how-populist-parties-around-the-world-use-the-same-playbook-to-win-on-social-media-295561","bytes":180547,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"How populist parties around the world use the same playbook to win on social media","by":[{"n":"Daswin De Silva","r":"Professor & Discipline Lead, AI and Analytics, Co-Director, La Trobe AI Institute, La Trobe University"},{"n":"Miriam Tanti","r":"Deputy Dean Commercial Growth and Professor of Education and Co-Director, La Trobe AI Institute, La Trobe University"}],"pub":"2026-11-17T23:46:33Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Not too long ago, when politicians wanted to reach the public they had to get past journalists, editors and producers, who collectively decided what made the news. But now, politicians can post, livestream, and message voters directly on social media platforms."]},{"k":"p","s":["On platforms such as Instagram, Tiktok and YouTube, ",["engagement algorithms","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c747x7gz249o"]," designed to ",["hold viewers’ attention","https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/commission-preliminarily-finds-tiktoks-addictive-design-breach-digital-services-act"]," determine what voters see and how often."]},{"k":"p","s":["The shift to algorithmic social media has been great for populist politicians around the world, who position themselves as representing the “will of the people”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In our recent research (under peer review), we collected 35,500 public posts from TikTok, Instagram, Facebook and YouTube, posted during elections by parties and politicians across the political spectrum in several countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Based on our analysis of this data, we found the “social media playbook” shared by many populist parties has four basic moves:"]},{"k":"li","s":["build an audience"]},{"k":"li","s":["provoke the audience"]},{"k":"li","s":["encourage the audience to spread the message"]},{"k":"li","s":["listen to the audience and give them more of what they want."]},{"k":"p","s":["These moves use the rules of social media platforms to gain attention, support and votes."]},{"k":"h","s":["What is populism?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Populism is a word that gets ",["thrown around a lot","https://doi.org/10.1017/psrm.2021.44"],", and is often used as a euphemism for ",["the far right","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0263395720955036"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["On a more careful view, ",["the defining feature of populism","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1477-7053.2004.00135.x"]," is it splits society into “the pure people” and “the corrupt elite”. It holds that politics should express the “general will” of the people."]},{"k":"p","s":["Populism is a thin ideology that can come from the left or the right. It can be combined with fuller ideologies from nationalism to socialism."]},{"k":"p","s":["Social media ",["fits well with populism","https://doi.org/10.1177/0163443718772192"],", as it gives populists a channel to invoke the support of ",["people against the establishment","https://doi.org/10.1177/0163443720957889"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Platform populists around the world"]},{"k":"p","s":["Populists across the globe have made the most of social media. In Argentina, the rise of Javier Milei to presidency in 2023 is partly attributed to ",["his TikTok account of 1.5 million followers","https://doi.org/10.1177/20563051261463361"],". In Germany, two populist parties, ",["both","https://comm.ku.dk/news/2026/afd-dominated-tiktok-in-the-german-election/"]," with a ",["highly visible","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/3/4/just-the-beginning-leftist-leader-heidi-reichinnek-woos-young-germans"]," TikTok presence, ",["doubled their vote","https://bundeswahlleiterin.de/en/info/presse/mitteilungen/bundestagswahl-2025/29_25_endgueltiges-ergebnis.html"]," at the 2025 election."]},{"k":"p","s":["In New York, Zohran Mamdani rose ",["from 1% in early polls to mayor in 2025","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/why-zohran-mamdanis-victory-matters-how-it-happened-what-it-means/"],", helped by ",["short videos on TikTok and Instagram","https://www.brookings.edu/articles/why-zohran-mamdanis-victory-matters-how-it-happened-what-it-means/"],". In the United Kingdom, Nigel Farage entered Parliament in 2024 ",["with more than 800,000 highly engaged TikTok followers","https://thedrum.com/news/2024/07/03/which-party-has-run-best-campaign-uk-s-first-tiktok-election"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["And in Australia, One Nation, ",["dismissed for decades as a fringe party","https://www.abc.net.au/listen/programs/the-making-of-one-nation"],", has polled 24-31% on primary votes this year. Its rise has been accompanied by ",["cartoons","https://theconversation.com/make-em-laugh-how-one-nation-uses-comedy-to-normalise-harmful-ideas-291658"]," and ",["AI-generated ads","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-26/one-nation-s-ai-ads-put-under-the-spotlight/107310848"]," with millions of views on social media."]},{"k":"p","s":["These parties and leaders differ sharply in politics, but their methods are remarkably alike."]},{"k":"h","s":["The social media playbook"]},{"k":"p","s":["Our research found a social media playbook of four moves: build, provoke, spread, listen."]},{"k":"p","s":["The first move is to “build” audiences across several platforms, using free accounts, follower networks and livestreaming. The leader is the brand, and short-form videos filmed with front-facing cameras project apparent authenticity. Building may also involve values-based content that makes the party seem reasonable and relatable."]},{"k":"p","s":["The second move is to “provoke” by simplifying issues, finding people to blame, and getting headlines. Short posts in simple language will make bold claims about complex problems such as housing or cost of living. Often the “corrupt elite”, the media or outsiders are blamed. Part of the goal here is to push the stories from ",["social media into mainstream news","https://academic.oup.com/book/8696"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The third move is to “spread” the message via followers. Share buttons, comment threads and tools such as TikTok’s duet and stitch make every follower a potential broadcaster. The platform’s engagement algorithms will then push the message to new audiences."]},{"k":"p","s":["Finally, “listen” uses audience engagement as live opinion polling. Platform analytics show which posts land within minutes, and ad tools can test different versions of a message. Whatever draws the most reactions becomes the next message, or even the next policy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Artificial intelligence (AI) is a big part of the playbook. The platform engagement algorithms are a kind of AI, and generative AI tools for producing text, images and video make all the moves cheaper and faster. A single video can be cut, captioned and translated for diverse demographics, and millions of likes, comments, views sorted by mood for targeted campaigns in minutes."]},{"k":"p","s":["An adversary can be made vivid with a synthetic image, as ",["One Nation’s AI-made Victorian election ad shows","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-12/one-nation-denies-victorian-election-campaign-video-racist/107254888"],". AI agents can even run accounts that post, reply and share around the clock, making a few supporters look like many."]},{"k":"h","s":["What we can do"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Mainstream parties struggle","https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3543507.3583267"]," with copying these moves because complex policies don’t fit into 15-second clips, and sound scripted beside a leader talking straight to the camera. Also, parties chasing broad support may avoid sharp messages that rally one group but alienate another."]},{"k":"p","s":["Platforms profit from this populist playbook, as their algorithms amplify the kind of messaging that keeps people scrolling and drives advertising revenue. Attempts to make platforms offer a simpler, chronological feed of posts – such as the ",["Australian government’s “My Feed, My Way” bill","https://theconversation.com/australias-digital-duty-of-care-was-just-introduced-to-parliament-so-whats-in-the-bill-294464"]," – would weaken part of this approach, but most of it would still work."]},{"k":"p","s":["That is why media literacy matters now and into the future, especially as AI blurs what’s real."]},{"k":"p","s":["This populist approach works by getting people to play along. So next time you see a post that makes you angry or hands you a villain, pause for a moment. Ask who made it, why, and what has been left out. You might have to look up some ",["other credible sources","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/016146811912101102"]," on the topic – not just ",["asking a chatbot","https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2322823121"]," – and gain a broader knowledge."]},{"k":"p","s":["Engagement algorithms are trained on our reactions. The most effective thing you can do is to stop and think before you hit share."]}],"chars":5775}};
